#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} &= \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx + 1 - 1 &= \\frac{1}{2} - 1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot (x - 2) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 5x \\\\\n3x - 5x &= 6 \\\\\n- 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 1 &= x + 1 \\\\\n2x - x &= 1 + 1 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dddef715-15d1-5688-9498-02a18f5a82ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058849} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc72d-60dc-598a-9e50-9f1f78d315f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058850} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4db2de-8f65-5588-b118-1aba6c4f87a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058851} {:answer/id #uuid "30236ad8-46b9-5497-95